    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003ePork shoulder is the right cut and the cheap one at the same time.\u003c/strong\u003e It needs the long braise to break down, which means the budget choice is also the correct choice.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003ePotatoes, carrots, and canned tomatoes are doing the volume work.\u003c/strong\u003e That is the whole trick of this dish. The meat sets the flavor and the cheap stuff sets the portion size.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned chickpeas are the quiet protein add.\u003c/strong\u003e They cost almost nothing per can and they soak up the tomato sauce better than anything else in the pot.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eRaisins sound strange and are not optional.\u003c/strong\u003e They melt into the sauce and give it the faint sweetness that makes menudo taste like menudo rather than like generic pork stew."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cut the pork shoulder into cubes and season with salt and black pepper.",
                    "Heat the vegetable oil in a large pot, stir in the achiote paste until it colors the oil, then brown the pork in batches.",
                    "Add the diced yellow onion and garlic and cook until softened.",
                    "Stir in the diced tomatoes and fish sauce, bring to a simmer, cover, and cook until the pork is starting to turn tender.",
                    "Add the cubed potatoes, carrots, and chicken livers and simmer uncovered until the vegetables are cooked through and the sauce reduces.",
                    "Fold in the chickpeas, diced green and red bell peppers, and golden raisins, and cook just until the peppers soften but keep some bite.",
                    "Taste and adjust the salt, then cook rice separately to serve underneath."
                ],
    "portion_html":  "Weigh the rice and the menudo in separate pots, subtracting the empty-pot weight from Step 1 each time, and divide each by 14. Even rice base first, even ladle of stew second. Every bowl comes in at \u003cstrong\u003e592 calories with 41 grams of protein\u003c/strong\u003e. This one reheats particularly well because the potatoes keep drinking up the sauce.",
